-- allium: 3
-- crm.allium
-- Scope: CRM domain model for JobSync — Person management,
--        Interview tracking, Task/Reminder automation, Activity Timeline,
--        Blocklist suppression, communication integration readiness
-- Includes: Person, Interview, Task, ActivityLog, Blocklist,
--           ConnectedAccount (deferred), domain events, GDPR fields on Person
-- Extends: crm-workflow.allium (Job Status Workflow, Kanban, JobStatusHistory)
-- Excludes:
--   - Job Status state machine (covered by crm-workflow.allium)
--   - GDPR Data Subject Requests (covered by crm-gdpr.allium)
--   - Connector transport (covered by future communication/calendar connector specs)
--   - UI layout (design agent responsibility)
--   - Vacancy pipeline (covered by vacancy-pipeline.allium)
-- Dependencies:
--   - Job, Company from shared-entities.allium
--   - JobStatusChanged from event-bus.allium
-- W-H1 (2026-08-25): this module NO LONGER depends on crm-gdpr.allium — the
-- dependency direction was flipped. crm-gdpr.allium now does `use "./crm.allium"
-- as crm` and references crm/Person et al. directly instead of keeping its own
-- `external entity` hand-copies of them. The old `use "./crm-gdpr.allium" as
-- gdpr` line here had ZERO qualified references (every cross-spec link in this
-- file is a prose comment), so removing it costs nothing and clears what would
-- otherwise have been a real import cycle. Do NOT re-add it: crm.allium is the
-- lower layer and must not import its own GDPR consumer.

use "./crm-workflow.allium" as workflow
use "./event-bus.allium" as events
use "./shared-entities.allium" as shared
use "./notification-dispatch.allium" as notifications

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

external entity User {
    -- Authenticated user from session (auth-session.allium).
    -- All CRM operations are scoped by user_id (ADR-015).
}

external entity Job {
    -- Job entity from job-aggregate.allium.
    user_id: String
    title: String
    company: Company
    status: String
}

external entity Company {
    -- Lookup entity from shared-entities.allium.
    label: String
    value: String
    domain: String?
    created_by: User
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

value FullName {
    first_name: String?
    last_name: String?
}

value TypedEmail {
    email: String
    type: ContactChannelType
    is_primary: Boolean
}

value TypedPhone {
    number: String
    type: ContactChannelType
    is_primary: Boolean
}

value Address {
    street: String?
    city: String?
    postal_code: String?
    country: String?
    -- Structured ISO codes for Holiday/Calendar/Geo integration
    -- Populated via CountrySelect/SubdivisionSelect UI components
    -- Free-text 'country' retained for display/legacy
    country_code: String?
    subdivision_code: String?
}

value CompanyAssociation {
    -- Links a Person to a Company with their position there and temporal
    -- context. A Person may hold positions at multiple companies simultaneously
    -- (e.g. recruiter at staffing agency + lecturer at university).
    -- `position` is a free-text job title / position at the company — a
    -- DIFFERENT concept from JobContact.role (the person's function in a hiring
    -- process, a controlled JobContactRole). Positions are open-ended
    -- ("VP Engineering", "Lecturer"), hence free text, not an enum.
    company: Company
    position: String?
    is_primary: Boolean
    start_date: Timestamp?
    end_date: Timestamp?
}

value SocialProfile {
    -- A link to a social/professional network profile.
    platform: SocialPlatform
    -- W-H3: url must be a web link (http/https scheme only) — a domain-level
    -- constraint validated at the create/update boundary. platform membership is
    -- enforced by the SocialPlatform enum type.
    url: String
}

value ActorMetadata {
    -- Tracks the origin of a record creation or modification.
    source: ActorSource
    name: String?
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract TimelineProjection {
    -- The Activity Timeline is a materialised read model.
    -- Consumers project domain events into ActivityLog entries.

    project: (activity_type: ActivityType, target_person: Person?, target_company: Company?, target_job: Job?, details: String?) -> ActivityLog

    @invariant AppendOnly
        -- ActivityLog entries are never updated after creation.

    @guidance
        -- Implementations should batch-insert timeline entries
        -- when processing event bursts (e.g. bulk status changes).
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

enum ActorSource {
    manual | import | api | system | workflow | email | calendar
}

enum ActorType {
    -- Who/what last updated a CRM record (Welle 3 Gap-7, ADR-035). Distinct from
    -- ActorSource (a data-source channel): this is the editor's identity class.
    user        -- a JobSync account holder
    automation  -- an automation / sync (ROADMAP 1.12), not a user
    self        -- the data subject editing their own record (ROADMAP 9.5), no account
}

enum JobContactRole {
    -- The person's function in a hiring process for a specific Job (the
    -- JobContact link). A closed, classifiable set — powers role badges
    -- (ROADMAP 2244) and warm-path / referral filtering (inside-track.allium).
    -- Distinct from CompanyAssociation.position (a free-text job title).
    -- A null JobContact.role means "unspecified" — there is deliberately NO
    -- `other` member: a role that doesn't fit is a signal to add a value here,
    -- not to hide it behind a catch-all (Allium: force ambiguity into the open).
    recruiter | hiring_manager | hr | referral | tipster | interviewer | decision_maker
}

enum ContactChannelType {
    work | home | other
}

enum DataSource {
    -- How the Person record entered the system. ONE provenance axis, not a set
    -- of parallel governance flags — a new origin earns a value here.
    --   manual        a full CRM create (PersonDirectory)
    --   auto_created  email/calendar sync saw an unknown participant
    --   imported      bulk import (5.7/5.8)
    --   quick_capture a minimal inline create from an entity picker
    -- quick_capture is behaviourally distinct from `manual` (the record is
    -- knowingly incomplete at birth) and from `auto_created` (a user chose to
    -- create it). Consumers that must treat a knowingly-incomplete contact
    -- differently read THIS value; deriving origin from field emptiness is
    -- wrong, because the first edit that fills a field erases the evidence.
    manual | auto_created | imported | quick_capture
}

enum ProcessingBasis {
    -- DSGVO Art. 6(1) legal basis for processing personal data.
    legitimate_interest | consent | contract
}

enum PersonStatus {
    active | archived | anonymized
}

enum InterviewStatus {
    scheduled | completed | cancelled | rescheduled
}

enum InterviewOutcome {
    pending | passed | rejected | waitlisted
}

enum TaskStatus {
    pending | in_progress | done | cancelled
}

enum ActivityType {
    status_changed | note_added | task_created | task_completed
    | interview_scheduled | interview_completed
    | contact_created | contact_updated | contact_deleted
    | email_sent | email_received | call_logged
    | document_attached | reminder_triggered
    | follow_up_sent | application_submitted
    | automation_degraded
    -- Inside Track referrals (spec: inside-track.allium). Deliberately NOT
    -- folded into status_changed, which means "a Job's status changed": a
    -- referral runs its own lifecycle, and merging them would make the two
    -- indistinguishable on a timeline that shows both.
    | referral_recorded | referral_status_changed
}

enum SocialPlatform {
    linkedin | xing | github | twitter | other
}

enum BlocklistType {
    -- How a Blocklist entry's handle is matched against a person's handles
    -- (Welle 3 Gap-6). Kept in step with crm-gdpr.allium's BlocklistType, which
    -- carries the same four members and the same semantics.
    email      -- exact email handle
    phone      -- exact phone handle
    domain     -- the email's domain or any parent domain (acme.com ⊇ eu.acme.com)
    pattern    -- a `*`-glob over the handle (ReDoS-safe matching)
}

enum ConnectedAccountProvider {
    gmail | outlook | caldav | imap
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

entity Person {
    -- The core CRM entity: a contact the user interacts with
    -- during their job search (recruiters, hiring managers, referrals).

    user_id: String
    name: FullName
    emails: List<TypedEmail>
    phones: List<TypedPhone>
    headline: String?
    social_profiles: List<SocialProfile>
    avatar_url: String?
    address: Address?

    companies: List<CompanyAssociation>
    status: PersonStatus

    -- Data governance (GDPR — see crm-gdpr.allium for DSR handling)
    data_source: DataSource
    processing_basis: ProcessingBasis
    retention_expires_at: Timestamp?
    -- Art. 7(3): the moment consent was withdrawn, or null if it never was.
    -- Only meaningful together with processing_basis = consent; a withdrawal
    -- marker on a legitimate_interest or contract record would be meaningless,
    -- since those bases are unaffected by withdrawal (crm-gdpr.allium
    -- ConsentBlockOnlyOnConsentBasis). Processing performed BEFORE withdrawal
    -- stays lawful and is not undone. Reversible — see crm-gdpr.allium rules
    -- WithdrawConsent / ReinstateConsent. Prisma: Person.consentWithdrawnAt.
    -- W-H1: lived on crm-gdpr's PersonGdprExtension until 2026-08-25; it was
    -- the one governance field that existed in Prisma and in code but had no
    -- home on this entity.
    consent_withdrawn_at: Timestamp?

    created_by: ActorMetadata
    updated_by: ActorMetadata
    created_at: Timestamp
    updated_at: Timestamp

    -- Relationships
    interviews: Interview with person = this
    job_contacts: JobContact with person = this
    task_targets: TaskTarget with target_person = this
    note_targets: NoteTarget with target_person = this
    timeline_activities: ActivityLog with target_person = this

    -- Derived
    primary_email: emails where is_primary = true -> email
    primary_company: companies where is_primary = true -> company
    is_auto_created: data_source = auto_created
    is_retention_expired: retention_expires_at != null and retention_expires_at <= now
    -- Art. 7(3): a consent-based record whose consent has been withdrawn is
    -- processing-restricted. Records on other legal bases are never blocked.
    -- The restriction itself (what may and may not act on such a record) is
    -- specified in crm-gdpr.allium ConsentBlockedRecordIsProcessingRestricted.
    is_consent_blocked: processing_basis = consent and consent_withdrawn_at != null

    transitions status {
        active -> archived
        active -> anonymized
        archived -> active
        archived -> anonymized
        terminal: anonymized
    }

    -- W-B3: the retention clock this invariant guarantees exists is a
    -- LAST-ACTIVITY clock, not a creation clock — the period tracks necessity
    -- instead of approximating it. UpdatePerson re-bases retention_expires_at to
    -- now + the operator's configured period (src/lib/crm/retention-policy.ts
    -- touchPersonRetention), and changing the configured period re-bases every
    -- existing deadline by the delta, so shortening the period takes effect
    -- immediately rather than at each contact's next edit.
    --
    -- Coverage is PARTIAL: UpdatePerson is the only wired site. See the open
    -- question at the end of this module.
    invariant AutoCreatedHasRetention {
        data_source = auto_created implies retention_expires_at != null
    }
}

entity Interview {
    -- A scheduled or completed interview linked to a Job and optionally
    -- to a Person (the interviewer contact at the company).

    user_id: String
    job: Job
    person: Person?
    interview_date: Timestamp
    location: String?
    notes: String?
    status: InterviewStatus
    outcome: InterviewOutcome?
    outcome_notes: String?
    created_at: Timestamp
    updated_at: Timestamp
    -- Update-actor provenance (Gap-7, ADR-035): type + nullable id (NOT a FK —
    -- survives actor deletion; the editor may be a user, an automation, or the
    -- data subject themselves, who has no account).
    updated_by_type: ActorType?
    updated_by_id: String?

    transitions status {
        scheduled -> completed
        scheduled -> cancelled
        scheduled -> rescheduled
        rescheduled -> completed
        rescheduled -> cancelled
        rescheduled -> rescheduled
        terminal: completed, cancelled
    }
}

entity Task {
    -- A to-do item linked to one or more CRM entities
    -- via the polymorphic TaskTarget join entity.

    user_id: String
    title: String
    description: String?
    due_date: Timestamp?
    status: TaskStatus
    created_at: Timestamp
    updated_at: Timestamp
    completed_at: Timestamp?
    -- Update-actor provenance (Gap-7, ADR-035): see ActorType.
    updated_by_type: ActorType?
    updated_by_id: String?

    targets: TaskTarget with task = this

    is_overdue: due_date != null and due_date <= now and status != done and status != cancelled

    transitions status {
        pending -> in_progress
        pending -> done
        pending -> cancelled
        in_progress -> done
        in_progress -> cancelled
        terminal: done, cancelled
    }
}

entity TaskTarget {
    -- Polymorphic join: exactly one target must be set
    -- (ExactlyOneTaskTarget invariant).
    task: Task
    target_person: Person?
    target_company: Company?
    target_job: Job?
}

entity Note {
    -- Free-text note linked to CRM entities via NoteTarget.
    user_id: String
    title: String?
    body: String
    created_at: Timestamp
    updated_at: Timestamp
    -- Update-actor provenance (Gap-7, ADR-035): see ActorType.
    updated_by_type: ActorType?
    updated_by_id: String?
    targets: NoteTarget with note = this
}

entity NoteTarget {
    -- Polymorphic join: exactly one target must be set
    -- (ExactlyOneNoteTarget invariant).
    note: Note
    target_person: Person?
    target_company: Company?
    target_job: Job?
}

entity JobContact {
    -- Links a Person to a Job as a contact/stakeholder.
    -- N:M: one Job can have multiple contact persons (recruiter,
    -- hiring manager, referral), and one Person can be the
    -- contact for multiple Jobs.
    -- Pattern: same join-entity pattern as TaskTarget/NoteTarget.
    user_id: String
    job: Job
    person: Person
    -- Controlled vocabulary (JobContactRole); null = unspecified. Existing
    -- free-text role values are mapped on migration: known strings -> the
    -- matching JobContactRole, unmappable -> null (weed / implementation task).
    role: JobContactRole?
    created_at: Timestamp
}

entity ActivityLog {
    -- Immutable timeline entry. Materialised read model projected
    -- from domain events, never updated after creation.

    user_id: String
    activity_type: ActivityType
    happened_at: Timestamp
    actor_id: String?
    target_person: Person?
    target_company: Company?
    target_job: Job?
    details: String?
    linked_record_name: String?
}

entity Blocklist {
    -- Suppresses Person auto-creation for specific handles
    -- during email/calendar sync.
    user_id: String
    handle: String
    type: BlocklistType
    reason: String?
    created_at: Timestamp
}

entity ConnectedAccount {
    -- OAuth connection for email/calendar sync (future 1.7/1.12).
    -- No rules govern this entity yet; it is declared for
    -- structural completeness. See deferred CommunicationConnector.sync.
    user_id: String
    provider: ConnectedAccountProvider
    handle: String
    access_token: String
    refresh_token: String?
    iv: String
    is_sync_enabled: Boolean
    last_synced_at: Timestamp?
    auth_failed_at: Timestamp?
    created_at: Timestamp
    updated_at: Timestamp
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- GDPR retention.
    -- W-B3 (2026-08-26): this is now the DEFAULT, not a constant. It is the
    -- period used when the operator has not chosen one. PrivacySettings
    -- (src/models/userSettings.model.ts) exposes a bounded choice of
    -- 180 / 365 / 730 / 1095 days, defaulting to this value so an operator who
    -- never opens the setting keeps exactly the policy declared here. There is
    -- no "unlimited" member: Art. 5(1)(e) does not permit indefinite retention.
    -- The 1095-day ceiling matches the timeline-retention window, so a Person is
    -- never retained longer than its own timeline.
    auto_created_retention: Duration = 730.days
    -- W-H1 (2026-08-25): `timeline_retention: Duration = 1095.days` was DELETED
    -- here. No rule in this module consumed it — W-E6 had already found the
    -- duplication and settled for a "kept for reference only" comment, which is
    -- the same symptom-patching W-H1 exists to undo: a value two files must
    -- manually keep in step is a drift mechanism, not documentation. Timeline
    -- retention is specified where its rule lives. Note the owner is currently
    -- CONTESTED between crm-gdpr.allium (ExpireOldTimelineActivities) and
    -- gdpr-data-rights.allium (PurgeOldCrmActivityLogs), which disagree on
    -- anonymise-vs-hard-delete — see the open question at the end of
    -- crm-gdpr.allium. Do not re-add a third copy here to resolve that.

    -- Reminders
    interview_reminder_before: Duration = 24.hours
    -- W-E6: reserved for a future follow-up-scheduling rule; no rule consumes it yet.
    follow_up_default_delay: Duration = 7.days

    -- Limits
    max_persons_per_user: Integer = 10000
    max_tasks_per_user: Integer = 5000
    max_blocklist_entries: Integer = 1000
    -- W-E6: the connected-accounts cap is enforced in inside-track.allium's own
    -- config; this member is unused here.
    max_connected_accounts: Integer = 5
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- ==========================================================
-- Person Lifecycle
-- ==========================================================

rule CreatePerson {
    -- User manually creates a contact from the CRM directory.
    when: CreatePerson(user, name, emails, phones, companies?, headline?, social_profiles?, address?)

    requires: emails.count > 0
    requires: Persons where user_id = user.id -> count < config.max_persons_per_user

    ensures: Person.created(
        user_id: user.id,
        name: name,
        emails: emails,
        phones: phones,
        companies: companies,
        headline: headline,
        social_profiles: social_profiles,
        address: address,
        status: active,
        data_source: manual,
        processing_basis: legitimate_interest,
        created_by: ActorMetadata(source: manual, name: user.name),
        updated_by: ActorMetadata(source: manual, name: user.name),
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
    )

    ensures: ContactCreated(person: person, source: manual)
}

rule QuickCapturePerson {
    -- Inline minimal create from an entity picker embedded in another workflow
    -- (recording a tip, adding a network connection, attaching a contact to a
    -- Job) — the user never leaves the host screen.
    --
    -- Structurally this is CreatePerson with every optional field omitted, so
    -- it adds no new capability. It is a SEPARATE rule because the origin is
    -- the only thing that differs and provenance must be recorded at the write:
    -- a Person born here cannot be told apart from a full manual create
    -- afterwards (see DECIDED note in Open Questions).
    when: QuickCapturePerson(user, name, email)

    requires: Persons where user_id = user.id -> count < config.max_persons_per_user

    ensures: Person.created(
        user_id: user.id,
        name: name,
        emails: {TypedEmail(email: email, type: work, is_primary: true)},
        phones: {},
        companies: {},
        social_profiles: {},
        status: active,
        data_source: quick_capture,
        processing_basis: legitimate_interest,
        created_by: ActorMetadata(source: manual, name: user.name),
        updated_by: ActorMetadata(source: manual, name: user.name),
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
    )

    ensures: ContactCreated(person: person, source: quick_capture)

    @guidance
        -- created_by.source stays `manual` — ActorSource is the channel a human
        -- or system acted through, DataSource is how the record originated. A
        -- quick capture is a manual act with a distinct origin; keeping the two
        -- axes separate is why quick_capture did not need a new ActorSource.
        -- Deliberately NOT guarded by an existing-email check (unlike
        -- AutoCreatePersonFromEmail): the picker has just shown the user the
        -- matching contacts, so creating anyway is an informed choice.
        -- MergePersons is the remedy if it turns out to be a duplicate.
}

rule AutoCreatePersonFromEmail {
    -- Email sync creates a Person for a previously unseen participant.
    -- Suppressed if the handle appears in the user's Blocklist.
    when: AutoCreatePerson(user, email_handle, display_name?)

    requires: not exists Blocklist{user_id: user.id, handle: email_handle, type: email}
    requires: not exists Blocklist{user_id: user.id, type: domain,
        handle: extract_domain(email_handle)}
    -- W-F4: also suppress on a `pattern`-type glob entry that matches the handle.
    -- Glob matching is algorithmic, so it is a black box here. The `phone` mode
    -- does not apply to an email-triggered creation (no phone is present).
    requires: not matches_blocked_pattern(user.id, email_handle)
    requires: not exists Person{user_id: user.id, primary_email: email_handle}

    let domain = extract_domain(email_handle)
    let matching_company = find_company_by_domain(user.id, domain)
    let initial_companies = if matching_company != null:
        {CompanyAssociation(company: matching_company, position: null, is_primary: true, start_date: now, end_date: null)}
    else:
        {}

    ensures: Person.created(
        user_id: user.id,
        name: FullName(first_name: display_name, last_name: null),
        emails: {TypedEmail(email: email_handle, type: work, is_primary: true)},
        phones: {},
        companies: initial_companies,
        status: active,
        data_source: auto_created,
        processing_basis: legitimate_interest,
        retention_expires_at: now + config.auto_created_retention,
        created_by: ActorMetadata(source: email, name: null),
        updated_by: ActorMetadata(source: email, name: null),
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
    )

    ensures: ContactCreated(person: person, source: auto_created)
}

rule UpdatePerson {
    when: UpdatePerson(user, person, changes)

    requires: person.user_id = user.id
    requires: person.status = active
    -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP (2026-08-25) — Art. 7(3) processing restriction, now
    -- EXPRESSIBLE. This guard could not be written before the crm-gdpr.allium
    -- dependency flip: the predicate lived on that module's PersonGdprExtension
    -- stub, and importing crm-gdpr from here would have closed a cycle. With
    -- consent_withdrawn_at and the derived is_consent_blocked on Person itself,
    -- the constraint states locally. The property it enforces is specified by
    -- crm-gdpr.allium ConsentBlockedRecordIsProcessingRestricted, which asserted
    -- it in prose because it had no way to reach these rules.
    -- Enforced at src/actions/person.actions.ts:320 (updatePerson) — rejects
    -- with crm.errors.consentWithdrawn.
    requires: not person.is_consent_blocked

    ensures: person.updated_by = ActorMetadata(source: manual, name: user.name)
    ensures: person.updated_at = now
    -- W-E3: ContactUpdated has ONE shape everywhere — {person_id, user_id, job_id?}.
    -- job_id is present only at the JobContact sites, where it routes the entry
    -- onto the Job/Company timelines as well; a plain person edit omits it.
    ensures: ContactUpdated(person_id: person.id, user_id: user.id)

    @guidance
        -- When the update originates from a sync connector (email/calendar),
        -- the source should be email or calendar, not manual.
        -- The caller determines the ActorMetadata based on context.
}

rule ArchivePerson {
    when: ArchivePerson(user, person)

    requires: person.user_id = user.id
    requires: person.status = active

    ensures: person.status = archived
    ensures: person.updated_at = now
}

rule ReactivatePerson {
    when: ReactivatePerson(user, person)

    requires: person.user_id = user.id
    requires: person.status = archived

    ensures: person.status = active
    ensures: person.updated_at = now
}

rule AnonymizePerson {
    -- GDPR erasure: irreversibly anonymize a Person and cascade.
    when: AnonymizePerson(user, person)

    requires: person.user_id = user.id
    requires: person.status != anonymized

    ensures: person.status = anonymized
    ensures: person.name = FullName(first_name: null, last_name: null)
    ensures: person.emails = {}
    ensures: person.phones = {}
    ensures: person.companies = {}
    ensures: person.headline = null
    ensures: person.social_profiles = {}
    ensures: person.avatar_url = null
    ensures: person.address = null
    ensures: person.created_by = ActorMetadata(source: person.created_by.source, name: null)
    ensures: person.updated_by = ActorMetadata(source: system, name: null)
    ensures: person.updated_at = now

    ensures:
        for interview in person.interviews:
            interview.person = null
            interview.notes = null
            interview.outcome_notes = null

    ensures:
        for al in person.timeline_activities:
            al.target_person = null
            al.details = null
            al.linked_record_name = null

    ensures:
        for nt in person.note_targets:
            not exists nt

    ensures:
        for tt in person.task_targets:
            not exists tt

    ensures:
        for jc in person.job_contacts:
            not exists jc

    ensures:
        for bl in Blocklists where user_id = person.user_id:
            if person.emails.any(e => e.email = bl.handle) or person.emails.any(e => extract_domain(e.email) = bl.handle):
                not exists bl

    -- W-B3: the reason is supplied by the CALLER, so the audit row distinguishes
    -- a user-initiated erasure (`anonymized`) from an automatic retention expiry
    -- (`retention_expired`, from ExpireAutoCreatedPersons). The projection that
    -- consumes this event is PII-free by construction — it nulls both the person
    -- reference and linked_record_name — so the reason is the only thing that
    -- survives to say WHY the record went.
    ensures: ContactDeleted(person_id: person.id, user_id: person.user_id, reason: reason)

    @guidance
        -- Implementation should also anonymize MessageParticipant
        -- and CalendarEventParticipant per crm-gdpr.allium.
}

rule MergePersons {
    -- Merge two Person records. Winner absorbs loser's relationships.
    when: MergePersons(user, winner, loser)

    requires: winner.user_id = user.id
    requires: loser.user_id = user.id
    requires: winner.status = active
    requires: loser.status = active
    requires: winner != loser

    ensures:
        for interview in loser.interviews:
            interview.person = winner

    ensures:
        for tt in loser.task_targets:
            tt.target_person = winner

    ensures:
        for nt in loser.note_targets:
            nt.target_person = winner

    ensures:
        for jc in loser.job_contacts:
            jc.person = winner

    ensures:
        for al in loser.timeline_activities:
            al.target_person = winner

    -- Announced BEFORE the loser ceases to exist, so bounded contexts holding
    -- their own references to a Person can transfer them (see
    -- inside-track.allium MergeCascadesToInsideTrack). Without this, such
    -- references are left to raw referential actions — which silently null or
    -- cascade-delete them, losing data a merge is supposed to preserve.
    ensures: PersonsMerged(winner: winner, loser: loser)

    -- W-E2: the loser Person ceases to exist here, which RecordContactDeletion
    -- projects onto the timeline as a merge (reason: merged) — the same
    -- ContactDeleted trigger AnonymizePerson emits. Announced before removal so
    -- the projection can still resolve the loser.
    ensures: ContactDeleted(person_id: loser.id, user_id: loser.user_id, reason: merged)

    ensures: not exists loser

    @guidance
        -- Winner's field values take precedence.
        -- Loser's emails/phones are appended with is_primary = false.
        -- Loser's company associations are appended with is_primary = false.
        -- Every consumer of PersonsMerged must be applied ATOMICALLY with this
        -- rule. A partially-applied merge leaves other contexts referencing a
        -- Person that no longer exists.
        -- CONSEQUENCE, so `weed` does not read this as drift: PersonsMerged is
        -- an observable outcome, NOT necessarily an event-bus publication. The
        -- implementation satisfies it INLINE, inside the merge transaction —
        -- publishing it would hand the cascade to an async consumer running
        -- after the loser row is gone, which is precisely the atomicity this
        -- rule forbids. Expect no `PersonsMerged` in the event registry.
}

rule AddJobContact {
    -- Link a Person to a Job as a contact/stakeholder.
    when: AddJobContact(user, job, person, role?)

    requires: job.user_id = user.id
    requires: person.user_id = user.id
    requires: not exists JobContact{job, person}
    -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP — Art. 7(3): a new link that NAMES a consent-blocked record
    -- is new processing. Enforced at src/actions/jobContact.actions.ts:38
    -- (addJobContact). The inverse RemoveJobContact deliberately carries NO such
    -- guard, in the spec and in the code: unlinking reduces processing, and
    -- blocking it would strand existing links on a record the user is trying to
    -- disentangle. See UpdatePerson for why this became expressible.
    requires: not person.is_consent_blocked

    ensures: JobContact.created(
        user_id: user.id,
        job: job,
        person: person,
        role: role,
        created_at: now
    )
    ensures: ContactUpdated(person_id: person.id, user_id: user.id, job_id: job.id)
}

rule RemoveJobContact {
    -- Unlink a Person from a Job.
    when: RemoveJobContact(user, job_contact)

    requires: job_contact.user_id = user.id

    ensures: not exists job_contact
    ensures: ContactUpdated(person_id: job_contact.person.id, user_id: user.id, job_id: job_contact.job.id)
}

rule ExpireAutoCreatedPersons {
    -- Temporal: ERASE Persons whose retention has elapsed.
    --
    -- W-B3 (2026-08-26): this rule used to end in `archived`. That was not a
    -- retention outcome — an archived Person is still stored, still listed,
    -- still searched, still emitted by the Art. 15 export, and one click from
    -- restoration. Expiry that archives never ends the processing, so it did not
    -- discharge Art. 5(1)(e) storage limitation and the system could not
    -- demonstrate enforcement of its own declared policy under Art. 5(2).
    when: person: Person.is_retention_expired

    requires: person.status != anonymized
    requires: person.is_auto_created

    -- Delegate to the erasure rule rather than restating its post-state here.
    -- AnonymizePerson owns the cascade (and already records a system actor via
    -- person.updated_by = ActorMetadata(source: system, name: null)); copying it
    -- into this rule would be a second owner and therefore a drift mechanism.
    ensures: AnonymizePerson(system, person, reason: retention_expired)

    @guidance
        -- Idempotency is STRUCTURAL, not guarded: `anonymized` is a terminal
        -- status and this rule's `requires` excludes it, so the rule cannot fire
        -- twice on one Person.
        --
        -- W-B3 replaces the former W-E4 note, which described a timeline entry
        -- the implementation wrote as an idempotency guard. That entry has been
        -- DELETED: it carried `linked_record_name` — the person's full name —
        -- on a row whose happened_at defaulted to the erasure instant, so it
        -- started a fresh timeline-retention clock on exactly the name the
        -- expiry existed to retire. Expiry EXTENDED the name's life. The
        -- accountability row is now produced by the ContactDeleted projection,
        -- which is PII-free by construction.
        --
        -- PER-USER GATE, deliberately NOT expressed as a `requires:` above.
        -- The implementation skips a user whose PrivacySettings.crm_retention_enabled
        -- is false (src/lib/scheduler/crm-cron.ts via
        -- src/lib/crm/retention-policy.ts). It is stated here as prose rather
        -- than as a rule predicate because NO user-configurable setting has a
        -- spec representation anywhere in this codebase — the account-deletion
        -- cooling-off period is equally unspec'd — and minting a bespoke
        -- predicate form for this one rule would create spec machinery with no
        -- precedent to keep it honest. See the open question at the end of this
        -- module.
        --
        -- Disabled means the automatic erasure is SUSPENDED — NOT that retention
        -- is unlimited. The period remains the operator's declared policy,
        -- retention_expires_at is still written and still advanced by the
        -- last-activity clock, and the record keeps showing when it fell due;
        -- only the unattended act stops. The offered periods are bounded with no
        -- "never" member, because Art. 5(1)(e) does not permit indefinite
        -- retention and a menu item saying otherwise would re-create as a
        -- configuration the exact defect W-B3 removed.
}

-- ==========================================================
-- Interview Lifecycle
-- ==========================================================

rule ScheduleInterview {
    when: ScheduleInterview(user, job, interview_date, person?, location?, notes?)

    requires: job.user_id = user.id
    -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP — Art. 7(3). The interviewee is optional, so the guard is
    -- conditional: an interview with no Person attached is unaffected. Enforced
    -- at src/actions/crmInterview.actions.ts:75 (scheduleInterview), inside the
    -- `if (input.personId)` branch — the same shape. See UpdatePerson for why
    -- this became expressible.
    requires: person != null implies not person?.is_consent_blocked

    ensures:
        let interview = Interview.created(
            user_id: user.id,
            job: job,
            person: person,
            interview_date: interview_date,
            location: location,
            notes: notes,
            status: scheduled,
            created_at: now,
            updated_at: now
        )

    ensures: InterviewScheduled(interview: interview, job: job)

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: user.id,
        activity_type: interview_scheduled,
        happened_at: now,
        actor_id: user.id,
        target_job: job,
        target_person: person,
        details: null,
        linked_record_name: job.title
    )
}

rule CompleteInterview {
    when: CompleteInterview(user, interview, outcome, outcome_notes?)

    requires: interview.user_id = user.id
    requires: interview.status in {scheduled, rescheduled}

    ensures: interview.status = completed
    ensures: interview.outcome = outcome
    ensures: interview.outcome_notes = outcome_notes
    ensures: interview.updated_at = now

    ensures: InterviewCompleted(interview: interview, outcome: outcome)

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: user.id,
        activity_type: interview_completed,
        happened_at: now,
        actor_id: user.id,
        target_job: interview.job,
        target_person: interview.person,
        details: null,
        linked_record_name: interview.job.title
    )
}

rule CancelInterview {
    when: CancelInterview(user, interview)

    requires: interview.user_id = user.id
    requires: interview.status in {scheduled, rescheduled}

    ensures: interview.status = cancelled
    ensures: interview.updated_at = now
}

rule RescheduleInterview {
    when: RescheduleInterview(user, interview, new_date, new_location?)

    requires: interview.user_id = user.id
    requires: interview.status in {scheduled, rescheduled}

    ensures: interview.status = rescheduled
    ensures: interview.interview_date = new_date
    -- W-B3: an omitted new_location preserves the current one (matches UpdateNote),
    -- rather than nulling it.
    ensures:
        if new_location != null:
            interview.location = new_location
    ensures: interview.updated_at = now
}

rule InterviewReminder {
    -- Temporal: notify the user before an upcoming interview.
    when: interview: Interview.interview_date - config.interview_reminder_before <= now

    requires: interview.status in {scheduled, rescheduled}
    -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP — Art. 7(3): an automated reminder is active processing, so
    -- a consent-blocked interviewee is excluded. Enforced at
    -- src/lib/scheduler/crm-cron.ts:184. This was previously stated only in the
    -- W-E5 @guidance below, because the predicate was unreachable from this
    -- module; it is now a real guard and the guidance records that.
    requires: interview.person != null implies not interview.person?.is_consent_blocked

    ensures: ReminderTriggered(
        user_id: interview.user_id,
        reason: interview_upcoming,
        target_job: interview.job,
        target_person: interview.person
    )

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: interview.user_id,
        activity_type: reminder_triggered,
        happened_at: now,
        target_job: interview.job,
        target_person: interview.person,
        details: interview.id,
        linked_record_name: interview.job.title
    )

    @guidance
        -- W-E4: details carries the interview id; the temporal sweep queries it
        -- as an idempotency guard so a reminder fires at most once per 24h window.
        -- W-E5: the implementation additionally skips consent-blocked interviewees
        -- (crm-gdpr.allium ConsentBlockedRecordIsProcessingRestricted) — a person
        -- whose consent is withdrawn is excluded from this active flow.
        -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP: that skip is no longer only prose — it is the
        -- `interview.person != null implies not ...is_consent_blocked` guard
        -- above. Kept here for the pointer to the owning invariant.
}

-- ==========================================================
-- Task Lifecycle
-- ==========================================================

rule CreateTask {
    when: CreateTask(user, title, description?, due_date?, targets)

    requires: targets.count > 0
    requires: Tasks where user_id = user.id -> count < config.max_tasks_per_user
    -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP — Art. 7(3). Targets are polymorphic; only the person-typed
    -- ones can be consent-blocked, and ANY blocked target rejects the whole
    -- creation rather than being silently dropped. Enforced at
    -- src/actions/crmTask.actions.ts:81, in the per-target ownership loop, which
    -- returns crm.errors.consentWithdrawn for the first blocked target. See
    -- UpdatePerson for why this became expressible.
    requires: targets.all(t => t.person != null implies not t.person?.is_consent_blocked)

    ensures:
        let task = Task.created(
            user_id: user.id,
            title: title,
            description: description,
            due_date: due_date,
            status: pending,
            created_at: now,
            updated_at: now
        )

    ensures:
        for target in targets:
            TaskTarget.created(task: task, target_person: target.person, target_company: target.company, target_job: target.job)

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: user.id,
        activity_type: task_created,
        happened_at: now,
        actor_id: user.id,
        target_job: targets.first?.target_job,
        target_person: targets.first?.target_person,
        details: null,
        linked_record_name: title
    )
}

rule CompleteTask {
    when: CompleteTask(user, task)

    requires: task.user_id = user.id
    requires: task.status in {pending, in_progress}

    ensures: task.status = done
    ensures: task.completed_at = now
    ensures: task.updated_at = now

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: user.id,
        activity_type: task_completed,
        happened_at: now,
        actor_id: user.id,
        details: null,
        linked_record_name: task.title
    )
}

rule CancelTask {
    when: CancelTask(user, task)

    requires: task.user_id = user.id
    requires: task.status in {pending, in_progress}

    ensures: task.status = cancelled
    ensures: task.updated_at = now
}

rule StartTask {
    when: StartTask(user, task)

    requires: task.user_id = user.id
    requires: task.status = pending

    ensures: task.status = in_progress
    ensures: task.updated_at = now
}

rule DeleteTask {
    when: DeleteTask(user, task)

    requires: task.user_id = user.id
    requires: task.status in {done, cancelled}

    ensures: not exists task

    @guidance
        -- Hard delete beyond terminal status transitions.
        -- Only tasks that have reached a terminal state (done, cancelled) may be
        -- permanently removed. Active tasks must be cancelled first.
        -- TaskTargets cascade via the database FK constraint.
}

rule TaskOverdueReminder {
    -- Temporal: notify the user when a task passes its due date.
    when: task: Task.due_date <= now

    requires: task.status = pending or task.status = in_progress
    -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP — Art. 7(3), the TaskOverdueReminder half of the same rule
    -- as InterviewReminder above. Enforced at src/lib/scheduler/crm-cron.ts:261,
    -- which skips the task if ANY of its targets is a consent-blocked person.
    requires: task.targets.all(tt => tt.target_person != null implies not tt.target_person?.is_consent_blocked)

    ensures: ReminderTriggered(
        user_id: task.user_id,
        reason: task_overdue,
        target_job: null
    )

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: task.user_id,
        activity_type: reminder_triggered,
        happened_at: now,
        details: task.id,
        linked_record_name: task.title
    )

    @guidance
        -- W-E4: details carries the task id; the temporal sweep queries it as an
        -- idempotency guard so an overdue reminder fires at most once per 24h window.
        -- W-E5: the implementation additionally skips consent-blocked task targets
        -- (crm-gdpr.allium ConsentBlockedRecordIsProcessingRestricted).
        -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP: now expressed as the `task.targets.all(...)` guard
        -- above rather than only in prose.
}

-- ==========================================================
-- Note Lifecycle
-- ==========================================================

rule CreateNote {
    when: CreateNote(user, title?, body, targets)

    requires: targets.count > 0
    -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP — Art. 7(3), same shape as CreateTask. Enforced at
    -- src/actions/crmNote.actions.ts:69. NOTE the asymmetry with UpdateNote and
    -- DeleteNote, which are NOT guarded here because the code does not guard
    -- them either (crmNote.actions.ts checks consent only in createNote). That
    -- may be a real gap — editing a note that names a consent-blocked person is
    -- arguably further processing — but it is a CODE gap, and inventing spec
    -- coverage for it would re-create exactly the spec-ahead-of-code drift W-H1
    -- was cleaning up. Raised as an open question below instead.
    requires: targets.all(t => t.person != null implies not t.person?.is_consent_blocked)

    ensures:
        let note = Note.created(
            user_id: user.id,
            title: title,
            body: body,
            created_at: now,
            updated_at: now
        )

    ensures:
        for target in targets:
            NoteTarget.created(note: note, target_person: target.person, target_company: target.company, target_job: target.job)

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: user.id,
        activity_type: note_added,
        happened_at: now,
        actor_id: user.id,
        target_person: targets.first?.target_person,
        target_job: targets.first?.target_job,
        details: null,
        linked_record_name: title
    )
}

rule UpdateNote {
    when: UpdateNote(user, note, title?, body?)

    requires: note.user_id = user.id

    ensures:
        if title != null:
            note.title = title
        if body != null:
            note.body = body
    ensures: note.updated_at = now
}

rule DeleteNote {
    when: DeleteNote(user, note)

    requires: note.user_id = user.id

    ensures: not exists note

    @guidance
        -- Hard delete: removes the Note and its NoteTarget join entities.
        -- NoteTargets cascade via the database FK constraint.
}

-- ==========================================================
-- Activity Timeline
-- ==========================================================

rule RecordStatusChange {
    -- Chain from JobStatusChanged events published by crm-workflow.
    when: JobStatusChanged(job_id, user_id, previous_status, new_status)

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: user_id,
        activity_type: status_changed,
        happened_at: now,
        actor_id: user_id,
        target_job: Job{id: job_id},
        details: null,
        linked_record_name: null
    )

    @guidance
        -- The details field may carry a JSON object with
        -- previous_status and new_status for rendering.
}

rule RecordContactCreation {
    -- W-E1: project ContactCreated events into the Activity Timeline. The linked
    -- record name is the Person's own name, so it is erased together with the
    -- Person by the anonymise cascade (crm-gdpr.allium timeline treatment) —
    -- there is no separate retention obligation on this field.
    when: ContactCreated(person, source)

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: person.user_id,
        activity_type: contact_created,
        happened_at: now,
        actor_id: person.user_id,
        target_person: person,
        details: null,
        linked_record_name: person.name.first_name
    )
}

rule RecordContactUpdate {
    -- W-E1: project ContactUpdated events into the Activity Timeline. A job↔person
    -- link/unlink carries job_id, which also surfaces the entry on the Job and
    -- Company timelines; a plain person edit omits it (target_job stays null).
    when: ContactUpdated(person_id, user_id, job_id?)

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: user_id,
        activity_type: contact_updated,
        happened_at: now,
        actor_id: user_id,
        target_person: Person{id: person_id},
        target_job: if job_id != null: Job{id: job_id} else: null,
        details: null,
        linked_record_name: null
    )
}

rule RecordContactDeletion {
    -- Project ContactDeleted events into the Activity Timeline.
    -- Fired by AnonymizePerson (reason: anonymized) and
    -- MergePersons (reason: merged). G9 fix.
    when: ContactDeleted(person_id, user_id, reason)

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: user_id,
        activity_type: contact_deleted,
        happened_at: now,
        actor_id: user_id,
        target_person: null,
        details: reason,
        linked_record_name: null
    )

    @guidance
        -- target_person is null because the Person record has been
        -- deleted/anonymized by the time this rule fires.
        -- The reason field (anonymized/merged/deleted) is stored in
        -- details as JSON for audit trail rendering.
}

rule RecordVacancyPromotion {
    -- Project VacancyPromoted events into the Activity Timeline. Promoting a
    -- discovered vacancy into a tracked Job IS the application-submitted moment,
    -- so the timeline entry is application_submitted (there is no separate
    -- vacancy_promoted activity type — that name belongs only to the
    -- notification channel, a different concern).
    when: VacancyPromoted(staged_vacancy_id, job_id, user_id)

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: user_id,
        activity_type: application_submitted,
        happened_at: now,
        actor_id: user_id,
        target_job: Job{id: job_id},
        details: null,
        linked_record_name: null
    )
}

rule RecordAutomationDegradation {
    -- Project AutomationDegraded events into the Activity Timeline.
    when: AutomationDegraded(automation_id, user_id, reason)

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: user_id,
        activity_type: automation_degraded,
        happened_at: now,
        actor_id: null,
        target_job: null,
        details: reason,
        linked_record_name: null
    )

    @guidance
        -- The actor_id is null because degradation is a system event
        -- (circuit breaker trip, auth failure, consecutive run failures).
}

rule RecordReferralRecorded {
    -- Project ReferralRecorded events (inside-track.allium) into the Activity
    -- Timeline, so a referral's pre-conversion life is visible and not only the
    -- Job it eventually reifies into.
    --
    -- Consumed UNQUALIFIED, like JobStatusChanged / VacancyPromoted /
    -- AutomationDegraded: crm.allium subscribes to foreign events without
    -- importing their emitter, which keeps the dependency pointing
    -- inside-track -> crm and never back.
    when: ReferralRecorded(referral_id, user_id, kind, tipster_person_id, target_company_id)

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: user_id,
        activity_type: referral_recorded,
        happened_at: now,
        actor_id: user_id,
        target_person: if tipster_person_id != null: Person{id: tipster_person_id} else: null,
        target_company: if target_company_id != null: Company{id: target_company_id} else: null,
        target_job: null,
        details: referral_id,
        linked_record_name: null
    )

    @guidance
        -- BOTH targets are set when known, so one entry surfaces on the
        -- tipster's PersonTimeline and on the target company's CompanyTimeline.
        -- Those are the two questions a referral answers ("who opened this
        -- door", "how do I get into this company"), and ActivityLog places no
        -- exactly-one-target invariant on itself (unlike TaskTarget/NoteTarget).
        -- Either may be null: a pure market tip has no company, and the GDPR
        -- cascade severs the tipster link.
        -- details carries the referral identity, and may fold in `kind`, as a
        -- JSON object with camelCase keys — the shape RecordStatusChange and
        -- RecordAutomationDegradation already use.
        -- The timeline must render a LOCALISED label derived from those values.
        -- Presenting details verbatim is a divergence: it puts a raw payload in
        -- front of the user.
}

rule RecordReferralStatusChange {
    -- Project ReferralStatusChanged events (inside-track.allium) into the
    -- Activity Timeline: open -> engaged -> relayed -> in_review, plus decline,
    -- revive and the temporal stale sweep.
    when: ReferralStatusChanged(referral_id, user_id, previous_status, new_status,
        system_initiated, tipster_person_id, target_company_id)

    ensures: ActivityLog.created(
        user_id: user_id,
        activity_type: referral_status_changed,
        happened_at: now,
        actor_id: if system_initiated: null else: user_id,
        target_person: if tipster_person_id != null: Person{id: tipster_person_id} else: null,
        target_company: if target_company_id != null: Company{id: target_company_id} else: null,
        target_job: null,
        details: referral_id,
        linked_record_name: null
    )

    @guidance
        -- actor_id is null for the temporal stale sweep (nobody acted) and the
        -- owning user otherwise. RecordStatusChange draws the same line for a
        -- user-driven change; the system side of the split is asserted HERE
        -- rather than inherited, because no existing projection models an
        -- actor-less system event yet.
        -- The owning user is the best actor this projection can carry: a
        -- Referral records its editor in updated_by_type/updated_by_id, but
        -- every write so far sets them to the owner, so they add nothing until
        -- a non-owner can edit (the account-less tipster, ROADMAP 9.5).
        -- details carries the referral identity plus previousStatus/newStatus as
        -- a camelCase JSON object, matching RecordStatusChange. The timeline
        -- must render a LOCALISED step label derived from those values — one
        -- label per activity_type, or the entry shows an untranslated key.
        -- target_job stays null even on conversion: the reified Job gets its own
        -- JobStatusChanged entry via TipReifiesToJob.
}

-- ==========================================================
-- Blocklist
-- ==========================================================

rule AddToBlocklist {
    when: AddToBlocklist(user, handle, type, reason?)

    requires: not exists Blocklist{user_id: user.id, handle: handle}
    requires: Blocklists where user_id = user.id -> count < config.max_blocklist_entries

    ensures: Blocklist.created(
        user_id: user.id,
        handle: handle,
        type: type,
        reason: reason,
        created_at: now
    )

    @guidance
        -- After adding to blocklist, consider archiving any existing
        -- auto-created Person whose primary_email matches the handle.
}

rule RemoveFromBlocklist {
    when: RemoveFromBlocklist(user, entry)

    requires: entry.user_id = user.id

    ensures: not exists entry
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant ExactlyOneTaskTarget {
    for tt in TaskTargets:
        (tt.target_person != null and tt.target_company = null and tt.target_job = null)
        or (tt.target_person = null and tt.target_company != null and tt.target_job = null)
        or (tt.target_person = null and tt.target_company = null and tt.target_job != null)
}

invariant ExactlyOneNoteTarget {
    for nt in NoteTargets:
        (nt.target_person != null and nt.target_company = null and nt.target_job = null)
        or (nt.target_person = null and nt.target_company != null and nt.target_job = null)
        or (nt.target_person = null and nt.target_company = null and nt.target_job != null)
}

-- BlocklistSuppressesAutoCreation is NOT declared here. It lives in
-- crm-gdpr.allium, which owns auto-creation constraints and data-minimisation
-- rules by declared scope, and which documents all four match modes
-- (email | phone exact, domain incl. parents, pattern glob).
-- This spec carried a duplicate under the same name until weed W-F1
-- (2026-08-17) found the two had drifted into opposite enforcement models — a
-- retroactive "blocklisted implies archived" here, creation-time there — with
-- the retroactive reading describing behaviour no rule implements. One owner,
-- not two synchronised copies. crm.allium still ENFORCES the property, in
-- AutoCreatePersonFromEmail's `requires` guards; it just no longer restates it.

invariant NoteHasAtLeastOneTarget {
    -- W-D3/W-D6: a Note is always ABOUT something — it has no standalone
    -- identity, and every read of a note reaches it through one of its targets.
    -- CreateNote establishes this (`requires: targets.count > 0`) and the delete
    -- side upholds it: when a Person, Company or Job is deleted its NoteTargets
    -- go with it, and a note left with no target at all is removed in the same
    -- transaction rather than surviving as a body no surface can render.
    --
    -- Unlike the converted-referral guarantee (W-D2 on inside-track's
    -- TipReifiesToJob), this IS a single-moment, falsifiable predicate over
    -- current state, so it is expressed rather than left as prose.
    --
    -- There is deliberately NO Task counterpart. A Task may legitimately hold
    -- zero targets: TaskBoard lists a user's tasks unfiltered and renders the
    -- targetless case, the overdue reminder still fires for it, and DeleteTask
    -- permits a hard delete only from a terminal status. A task that loses its
    -- last target keeps existing as a commitment; it just loses its link.
    -- A NoteTarget whose three polymorphic references are all null is a ghost,
    -- not a target: databases seeded before migration 20260512224224 (when the
    -- FKs were still ON DELETE SET NULL) can hold such rows. It must not count.
    for note in Notes:
        note.targets where (target_person != null
            or target_company != null
            or target_job != null) -> count > 0
}

invariant AtMostOnePrimaryCompany {
    -- A Person may have zero or one primary company association.
    for person in Persons:
        person.companies where is_primary = true -> count <= 1
}

invariant PersonOwnedByUser {
    -- ADR-015: all Person records must have an owner.
    for person in Persons:
        person.user_id != null
}

invariant SubdivisionRequiresCountry {
    -- W-H2: an ISO 3166-2 subdivision code is namespaced by its country, so it
    -- is meaningless without one. Enforced at create AND update (a partial update
    -- must not null the country while leaving a subdivision set).
    for person in Persons:
        person.address?.subdivision_code != null implies person.address?.country_code != null
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor CrmUser {
    identified_by: User
}

actor Scheduler {
    -- System actor for temporal rules (reminders, retention).
    identified_by: User where role = system
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface PersonDirectory {
    facing user: CrmUser

    let persons = Persons where user_id = user.id

    exposes:
        for person in persons:
            person.name
            person.primary_email
            person.primary_company
            person.companies
            person.status
            person.data_source
            person.created_at

    provides:
        CreatePerson(user, name, emails, phones, companies?, headline?, social_profiles?, address?)
        MergePersons(user, winner, loser)

    related:
        PersonDetail(person)

    @guidance
        -- Full-text search across name, email, company.
        -- Filter by status, data_source, company.
}

surface PersonQuickCapture {
    -- The contact entity-picker. Not a page of its own: a boundary embedded in
    -- ANY host surface that needs to reference a Person (inside-track's
    -- TipCapture, the Job contact panel). It returns a selection to its host
    -- and, when nothing matches, lets the user mint a minimal contact in place
    -- rather than abandoning the host workflow.
    facing user: CrmUser

    let candidates = Persons where user_id = user.id and status = active

    exposes:
        for candidate in candidates:
            candidate.name
            candidate.primary_email
            candidate.primary_company

    provides:
        QuickCapturePerson(user, name, email)

    @guarantee MinimalCaptureIsProvenanced
        -- A contact minted at this boundary is recorded as
        -- data_source = quick_capture, so its incomplete-at-birth origin stays
        -- answerable for the life of the record. A host surface may not create a
        -- Person here by any other route, because an origin cannot be
        -- reconstructed after the fact.

    @guidance
        -- Search over name + email, as PersonDirectory does. Creation is offered
        -- only once the query has matched nothing, so selecting an existing
        -- contact stays the default path. The host surface decides what to do
        -- with the returned Person; linking it is the host's concern, not this
        -- boundary's. The company-side equivalent (create-on-type in the company
        -- picker) needs no provenance seam: Company is a lookup value with no
        -- data subject behind it.
}

surface PersonDetail {
    facing user: CrmUser
    context person: Person where user_id = user.id

    exposes:
        person.name
        person.emails
        person.phones
        person.headline
        person.social_profiles
        person.avatar_url
        person.address
        person.companies
        person.status
        person.data_source
        person.processing_basis
        person.retention_expires_at
        -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP: a surface whose `provides` guards read
        -- is_consent_blocked must also expose it, or the boundary hides WHY
        -- three controls are unavailable. crm-gdpr.allium surface
        -- ConsentManagement — rendered inside this one — exposes the same
        -- predicate alongside the withdraw/reinstate actions.
        person.is_consent_blocked
        person.created_at
        person.updated_at

        for interview in person.interviews:
            interview.job
            interview.interview_date
            interview.status
            interview.outcome

        for tt in person.task_targets:
            tt.task.title
            tt.task.status
            tt.task.due_date

        for jc in person.job_contacts:
            jc.job
            jc.job.title
            jc.job.company
            jc.job.status
            jc.role

    provides:
        -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP (2026-08-25) — the `not person.is_consent_blocked`
        -- halves below mirror the rules' new Art. 7(3) guards, so the boundary
        -- states the same condition the rule enforces rather than only hiding a
        -- control. Which operations carry it is NOT uniform, and the asymmetry
        -- is deliberate — it tracks what the code actually enforces:
        --   guarded   UpdatePerson, AddJobContact, CreateNote  (edits and new
        --             links = further processing)
        --   unguarded ArchivePerson, ReactivatePerson, AnonymizePerson,
        --             RemoveJobContact, UpdateNote, DeleteNote
        -- Anonymize and Archive must stay reachable on a blocked record — they
        -- are how a withdrawal is honoured, and crm-gdpr.allium
        -- ConsentBlockedRecordIsProcessingRestricted names erasure, export and
        -- reinstatement as the permitted operations. RemoveJobContact reduces
        -- processing. UpdateNote / DeleteNote are unguarded only because the
        -- code does not guard them — see the open question at the end of this
        -- file; do not "fix" that here without fixing the code in the same
        -- change.
        UpdatePerson(user, person, changes)
            when person.status = active
                and not person.is_consent_blocked
        ArchivePerson(user, person)
            when person.status = active
        ReactivatePerson(user, person)
            when person.status = archived
        AnonymizePerson(user, person)
            when person.status != anonymized
        AddJobContact(user, job, person, role?)
            when person.status = active
                and not person.is_consent_blocked
        RemoveJobContact(user, job_contact)
            when person.status = active
        CreateNote(user, title?, body, targets)
            when person.status = active
                and not person.is_consent_blocked
        UpdateNote(user, note, title?, body?)
            when person.status = active
        DeleteNote(user, note)
            when person.status = active

    related:
        PersonTimeline(person)

    @guidance
        -- Tabbed layout: Overview, Interviews, Tasks, Notes, Timeline, Related Jobs.
}

surface InterviewCalendar {
    facing user: CrmUser

    let interviews = Interviews where user_id = user.id

    exposes:
        for interview in interviews:
            interview.job
            interview.person
            interview.interview_date
            interview.location
            interview.status
            interview.outcome

    provides:
        ScheduleInterview(user, job, interview_date, person?, location?, notes?)
        CompleteInterview(user, interview, outcome, outcome_notes?)
            when interview.status in {scheduled, rescheduled}
        CancelInterview(user, interview)
            when interview.status in {scheduled, rescheduled}
        RescheduleInterview(user, interview, new_date, new_location?)
            when interview.status in {scheduled, rescheduled}

    timeout:
        InterviewReminder

    @guidance
        -- Calendar view with upcoming interviews highlighted.
}

surface TaskBoard {
    facing user: CrmUser

    let tasks = Tasks where user_id = user.id

    exposes:
        for task in tasks:
            task.title
            task.description
            task.due_date
            task.status
            task.is_overdue
            task.completed_at
            for target in task.targets:
                target.target_person
                target.target_company
                target.target_job

    provides:
        CreateTask(user, title, description?, due_date?, targets)
        StartTask(user, task)
            when task.status = pending
        CompleteTask(user, task)
            when task.status in {pending, in_progress}
        CancelTask(user, task)
            when task.status in {pending, in_progress}
        -- W-A2: DeleteTask is intentionally NOT surfaced here. Cancel is the
        -- terminal user action on the board; the DeleteTask rule remains a
        -- callable repository capability (terminal-guarded) for future bulk /
        -- API consumers, but no board button renders it today, so claiming it on
        -- this surface would over-state the boundary.

    timeout:
        TaskOverdueReminder

    @guidance
        -- Group by status. Highlight overdue tasks.
}

surface PersonTimeline {
    -- Activity feed scoped to a single Person.
    facing user: CrmUser
    context person: Person where user_id = user.id

    let activities = person.timeline_activities

    exposes:
        for activity in activities:
            activity.activity_type
            activity.happened_at
            activity.actor_id
            activity.target_job
            activity.details
            activity.linked_record_name

    contracts:
        fulfils TimelineProjection

    @guarantee ChronologicalOrder
        -- Activities are presented in reverse chronological order.

    @guidance
        -- Vertical timeline with icons per activity_type.
        -- Filter by activity_type.
}

surface JobTimeline {
    -- Activity feed scoped to a single Job.
    -- Shows status changes, interviews, notes, tasks linked to this job.
    facing user: CrmUser
    context job: Job where user_id = user.id

    let activities = ActivityLogs where target_job = job

    exposes:
        for activity in activities:
            activity.activity_type
            activity.happened_at
            activity.actor_id
            activity.target_person
            activity.details
            activity.linked_record_name

    contracts:
        fulfils TimelineProjection

    @guarantee ChronologicalOrder
        -- Activities are presented in reverse chronological order.

    @guidance
        -- Embeddable in JobDetail page.
        -- Merges CRM activity with JobStatusHistory for a unified view.
}

surface CompanyTimeline {
    -- Activity feed scoped to a single Company.
    -- Aggregates activities across all Persons and Jobs at this company.
    facing user: CrmUser
    context company: Company

    let activities = ActivityLogs where target_company = company

    exposes:
        for activity in activities:
            activity.activity_type
            activity.happened_at
            activity.actor_id
            activity.target_person
            activity.target_job
            activity.details
            activity.linked_record_name

    contracts:
        fulfils TimelineProjection

    @guarantee ChronologicalOrder
        -- Activities are presented in reverse chronological order.

    @guidance
        -- Embeddable in a future CompanyDetail page.
        -- Shows all CRM interactions with this company across all contacts and jobs.
}

surface BlocklistSettings {
    facing user: CrmUser

    let entries = Blocklists where user_id = user.id

    exposes:
        for entry in entries:
            entry.handle
            entry.type
            entry.reason
            entry.created_at

    provides:
        AddToBlocklist(user, handle, type, reason?)
        RemoveFromBlocklist(user, entry)

    @guidance
        -- Simple table in Settings. Filter by type.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Deferred Specifications
------------------------------------------------------------

deferred CommunicationConnector.sync       -- see future specs/communication-connector.allium (1.12)
deferred CalendarConnector.sync            -- see future specs/calendar-connector.allium (1.7)
deferred ContactExtraction.extract         -- NLP extraction from job descriptions (5.7)
deferred ImportExport.bulkImport           -- CSV import/export (5.8)

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Company remains a lookup value. Person now references multiple companies via CompanyAssociation (position, isPrimary, temporal bounds). Should Company gain independent lifecycle management in the future, or does the current lookup + association model suffice?"

open question "Should Tasks be standalone entities or always linked to a Job/Person? What about personal reminders?"

open question "How should Person merge handle conflicting GDPR processing_basis? Winner's basis takes precedence?"

open question "Should the Activity Timeline show ALL events or only 'important' ones? Is there a visibility filter?"

open question "Should interview scheduling integrate with external calendar immediately (1.7) or start with manual-only?"

open question "No surface provides AutoCreatePerson. This trigger depends on email/calendar sync (CommunicationConnector) which is deferred."

-- DECIDED (tend, 2026-08-17) — quick-capture provenance. Resolves the former
-- open question "should inline minimal creation be recorded as durable
-- provenance?" with YES, via candidate (b) of that question's own analysis:
--   * A new DataSource literal `quick_capture`, on the SAME axis as
--     manual | auto_created | imported. This reuses the existing provenance
--     machinery and the AutoCreatedHasRetention precedent (a data_source value
--     that implies governance) instead of inventing a parallel governance state.
--   * Witnessed by rule QuickCapturePerson and surface PersonQuickCapture.
--   * The sequencing note in the old question is now satisfied: the seam exists
--     BEFORE the first quick-create write, so no contact is born ambiguous.
-- Deliberately NOT specified here:
--   * "Completeness" stays a DERIVED notion (only name + one email present,
--     nothing else) and never a stored flag. No consumer derives it yet; the
--     first one that needs it adds the derived value.
--   * The consequences belong at their consumers, not on this enum: the
--     outreach gate (IT-8, communication-connector OutboundIsThirdPartyTransfer),
--     the completion nudge (ROADMAP 2.20), and bulk-import reuse (5.7/5.8 —
--     imported contacts are minimal too and must reuse THIS fact rather than
--     minting a second "incomplete contact" concept).

open question "Retention for quick_capture Persons. ExpireAutoCreatedPersons guards on is_auto_created, and AutoCreatedHasRetention constrains only auto_created — so a quick_capture Person today gets no retention_expires_at and never auto-archives, i.e. it is governed exactly like a full manual create. Is that the intent? A quick capture is a deliberate user act (unlike email-sync auto-creation), which argues for manual-like treatment; but it is also knowingly incomplete and may be a contact the user never returns to, which argues for a shorter leash. Decide in crm-gdpr.allium before quick-capture volume accumulates,  W-B3 (2026-08-26) makes this easier to answer without resolving it: retention is now a LAST-ACTIVITY clock rather than a creation clock, and a last-activity leash is a defensible default for BOTH sources — a quick capture the user keeps working with never falls due, and one they never return to is exactly the record the balancing test says should not still be held. That removes the sharpest edge of the question (a deliberate user act being erased on an arbitrary anniversary) but does not answer whether quick_capture should carry a leash at all."

open question "A referral timeline entry cannot deep-link to its Referral. ActivityLog has target_person / target_company / target_job only, and gaining a target_referral field would make crm.allium depend on inside-track.allium — the wrong direction (inside-track imports crm, not the reverse). RecordReferralRecorded / RecordReferralStatusChange therefore carry the referral identity in `details`, following RecordStatusChange's precedent, which means a timeline consumer must parse `details` to navigate. Acceptable, or is a polymorphic target seam on ActivityLog (mirroring TaskTarget/NoteTarget's nullable-FK pattern) worth introducing so any spec can project onto the timeline without crm knowing about it?"

open question "W-H1 FOLLOW-UP — Art. 7(3) coverage is UNEVEN across note lifecycle, and the spec now says so rather than papering over it. CreateNote rejects a consent-blocked target (src/actions/crmNote.actions.ts:69) but UpdateNote and DeleteNote do not check at all, so a note that names a consent-blocked person can still be edited after withdrawal. crm-gdpr.allium ConsentBlockedRecordIsProcessingRestricted says 'no field edits and no automated activity may act on a consent-blocked record', which reads as covering an edit to a note ABOUT that person; but it also says the restriction is 'a guard on the creating ACTION, not a standing predicate over existing links', which reads as excluding it. Both readings are available, which is the actual problem. Decide which, then make code and spec agree in ONE change: either add the guard to updateNote/deleteNote and to the rules here, or state explicitly in the invariant that edits to pre-existing notes stay permitted because the processing was already lawful when it began. Note DeleteNote is the awkward case for the strict reading — refusing to delete a note about a person who withdrew consent is the opposite of what Art. 7(3) wants."

open question "W-H1 FOLLOW-UP — three further consent-block enforcement sites are NOT reflected in any spec, and they are not this module's to add: src/actions/referral.actions.ts:51, src/actions/personConnection.actions.ts:56 and src/actions/warmPath.actions.ts:82,113,114 all call isConsentBlocked, and all three belong to inside-track.allium (Referral, PersonConnection, warm-path traversal), which imports crm.allium rather than the reverse. The warm-path ones are the interesting case: they `continue` past a blocked person rather than rejecting, i.e. a consent-blocked contact silently vanishes from warm-path results instead of producing an error — a different enforcement SHAPE from every other site, and one whose absence from the spec means nobody has judged whether silent omission is the right behaviour. Whoever next tends inside-track.allium should add the guards there; the predicate is crm/Person.is_consent_blocked and it is importable today."

open question "W-B3 FOLLOW-UP — the last-activity retention clock is wired at ONE site, so a Person can be actively worked with and still expire. UpdatePerson calls touchPersonRetention (src/lib/crm/retention-policy.ts); nothing else does. Adding a CrmNote or CrmTask that targets the Person, scheduling or completing a CrmInterview with them, linking them to a Job via JobContact, recording a PersonConnection, and every Referral touch (tipster/insider/forwarded_to) all leave the deadline untouched. So an auto-created recruiter you exchange notes and interviews with for two years is erased on the anniversary of the last time you edited their NAME — which is precisely the necessity test the clock was introduced to make real, failing in the case it most needs to hold. Analysis docs/wh-b3-retention-analysis.md section 4.7 lists these sites and says explicitly that WHICH of them count is a design decision, not a mechanical one, so it was deliberately not guessed. Decide the set, then wire it and state it on invariant AutoCreatedHasRetention. Note archivePerson is explicitly NOT a candidate: archiving is the opposite of \"still needed\"."

open question "W-B3 FOLLOW-UP — Person has no last-activity column, so the retention clock has no durable basis to recompute from. retention_expires_at is the only materialised deadline; when it is absent, rebaseCrmRetention falls back to Person.updated_at as a last-activity proxy. updated_at also bumps on system-driven cascade writes (anonymisation cascades, status sweeps, consent changes), so the proxy errs toward retaining LONGER than a true interaction clock would — the wrong direction for Art. 5(1)(e). The re-base on the primary path avoids the issue by shifting stored deadlines by the period delta rather than recomputing (which also keeps it idempotent: recomputing from updated_at would let repeated visits to the settings page walk the clock forward, since the re-base write itself bumps updated_at). A dedicated Person.last_activity_at column would remove the proxy entirely and make the clock auditable, which Art. 5(2) accountability would prefer; it needs a migration slot. Worth doing before an auto-creation writer lands and real deadlines start accumulating."

open question "W-B3 FOLLOW-UP — user-configurable settings have NO representation in any Allium module, so two live behavioural gates are invisible to allium check. ExpireAutoCreatedPersons is skipped entirely for a user whose PrivacySettings.crm_retention_enabled is false, and the retention period itself is a per-user choice of 180/365/730/1095 days rather than config.auto_created_retention; both are stated only in that rule's @guidance. The account-deletion cooling-off period (PrivacySettings.cooling_off_days, specs/gdpr-data-rights.allium) is equally unspec\u0027d, so this is a pattern, not a one-off. Minting a bespoke predicate form for a single rule was rejected as spec machinery with no precedent to keep it honest. The real question is whether Allium should model UserSettings as an entity that rules may read — which would make every settings-gated behaviour checkable — or whether user configuration is deliberately out of scope for these specs, in which case say so once, centrally, instead of leaving each gate to prose."
